WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:03.000
-I'm so happy you're here. I'm such a big fan of yours.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.667
But I don't know what to call you.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:06.701
Do I call you Guillermo?

00:00:06.701 --> 00:00:10.100
Or do I call you... Doctor Del Toro?

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:11.667
-[ Laughs ] -You got your doctorate.

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:13.767
-That is my -- -Look at that.

00:00:13.767 --> 00:00:16.334
[ Cheers and applause ] Wow!

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:18.968
-Yes, indeed. -Now, where was this?

00:00:18.968 --> 00:00:21.534
Who gave this to you? -This is a --

00:00:21.534 --> 00:00:23.067
the best, the largest,

00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:27.300
the most famous university in Latin America.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.000
National University of Mexico.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.701
-Wow. -And -- And --

00:00:30.701 --> 00:00:32.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:32.801 --> 00:00:34.834
-You can only write prescriptions

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:37.601
if you have the hat, you know? [ Laughter ]

00:00:37.601 --> 00:00:39.334
-You have to wear the hat? -You have to wear the hat.

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.968
-You have to wear the hat. I'm so happy that you're here.

00:00:42.968 --> 00:00:46.501
Did your parents want you to be a doctor growing up,

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:48.467
or were they happy that you went into movies?

00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:50.667
-They were happy for us to have a pulse.

00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:51.968
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:53.534
They were like, "Oh, the kids are okay.

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:55.000
They're breathing."

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.767
-So they're alright. Yeah. -Yeah, they were.

00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:58.267
-When did you know?

00:00:58.267 --> 00:00:59.934
I read somewhere you were like 8 years old

00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:01.400
and you grabbed your dad's camera.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.334
-Yeah, my dad used to sell cars at a car dealership.

00:01:05.334 --> 00:01:08.934
And they would pay him with stuff, like, down payments.

00:01:08.934 --> 00:01:10.567
And they gave him a Super 8 camera,

00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:12.334
a projector, and a screen.

00:01:12.334 --> 00:01:15.467
And I started doing Super 8 movies with that.

00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:18.100
-And what -- Do you remember any of your movies you used to make?

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.801
-[ Laughs ] Well, early on, I did stop-motion,

00:01:19.801 --> 00:01:23.400
clay animation, about a serial-killer potato.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.267
[ Laughter ] That -- It became sentient.

00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:30.067
Murdered a whole family. Decided to conquer the world.

00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:31.601
-Why did the potato kill the family?

00:01:31.601 --> 00:01:33.834
-It was a really -- Hubris.

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:35.067
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:36.834
The potato wanted to conquer the world

00:01:36.834 --> 00:01:39.634
and stepped out into the street and got crushed by a car.

00:01:39.634 --> 00:01:41.267
[ Laughter ]

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.234
-Mashed potato at the end?

00:01:43.234 --> 00:01:45.300
-Mashed potato. -[ Laughs ] That's wild.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.968
-It ended in violence. -It ended in violence. Yeah.

00:01:47.968 --> 00:01:50.267
Can we talk about "Guillermo del Toro's Pinocchio"?

00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:53.100
-Yes, please. -What a masterpiece.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.033
-Thank you. -It's a triumph.

00:01:55.033 --> 00:01:58.968
Man, oh, man. This movie. [ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:01.400
Wait till you see --

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.100
And I want to know when you got the idea

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.300
that you wanted to do your version of "Pinocchio"

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.667
and that you wanted to make it stop-motion animation.

00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:10.300
-Well, stop motion, I've been doing it

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.367
since I was a kid, as I said.

00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:16.067
And the thing is, I felt that for the puppet

00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:19.501
to belong to the same world, everybody had to be a puppet.

00:02:19.501 --> 00:02:21.067
And it's a story about --

00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:23.467
It's set during the rise of Mussolini.

00:02:23.467 --> 00:02:25.601
And it's the only character

00:02:25.601 --> 00:02:27.834
that doesn't behave like a puppet is the puppet.

00:02:27.834 --> 00:02:30.334
He's a very free thinker.

00:02:30.334 --> 00:02:32.968
And we started the adventure with a shadow machine

00:02:32.968 --> 00:02:35.434
and my co-director, Mark Gustafson,

00:02:35.434 --> 00:02:40.000
and this is a titanic piece of animation.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.667
-Yeah. -Gigantic.

00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:42.934
-I love the story.

00:02:42.934 --> 00:02:45.200
Did you fall in love with it from the Disney cartoon?

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.567
-Yeah, I saw it with my mom.

00:02:46.567 --> 00:02:49.100
It was the second or third movie I saw with my mom.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:52.300
And I felt that was the only movie, at that age,

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.167
that captured how horrible childhood felt.

00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:57.067
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:59.267
And I was enchanted by that.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:01.334
I said, "That guy gets me."

00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:03.667
[ Laughter ] -Wow!

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:05.667
-And then I said --

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:09.167
My mom and I were very, very good friends growing up.

00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:12.667
She was the weird one in the family along with me.

00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:14.300
She would have worn that sweater, by the way.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.767
-She would have -- Yeah? That's -- Right?

00:03:16.767 --> 00:03:18.767
-She would have loved it. Yeah. Every Christmas.

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:21.334
-Your mom had a lot to do with this movie getting made.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:23.033
-Yeah, we talked about it.

00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:25.133
She would get me a Pinocchio every couple of years.

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:26.834
A wooden Pinocchio.

00:03:26.834 --> 00:03:30.467
She knew that I wanted to do it since I was very young.

00:03:30.467 --> 00:03:34.601
And, unfortunately, she passed away right before the movie

00:03:34.601 --> 00:03:37.000
premiered in the London Film Festival.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.234
But I still feel her in there. You know? She's --

00:03:41.234 --> 00:03:43.868
-I think she'd be very proud. -She would be happy.

00:03:43.868 --> 00:03:45.601
[ Cheers and applause ] She would be.

00:03:45.601 --> 00:03:48.968
-Uh, you, um...

00:03:48.968 --> 00:03:52.200
You brought us the star of the movie.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.834
-Yeah, two actors came with me.

00:03:53.834 --> 00:03:56.834
-Two actors come with you. This is -- I don't want to --

00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:59.601
-These are the real puppets.

00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:01.534
You can animate them, you know.

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.701
This is Geppetto. And this is --

00:04:07.701 --> 00:04:10.000
Mechanical. They're mechanical puppets.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.767
So all the mechanisms are in the face.

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:14.033
They are made of silicone.

00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:15.634
And inside, there's basically,

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:18.434
like, a Swiss watch of mechanics.

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:21.200
So you can animate them frame by frame.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:25.934
You know, look, for example, if you want him to be sad...

00:04:29.534 --> 00:04:32.400
And if he's worried...

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.367
He's worried about Pinocchio now. [ Laughs ]

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:39.067
If you want him to be angry, you know, he would get angry.

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:40.634
He's a pretty obedient guy.

00:04:40.634 --> 00:04:43.033
There you go.

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:45.667
That's his -- telling Pinocchio, "What are you doing?"

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:48.734
[ Laughter ] -How long does this take?

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:50.334
Because when I'm watching it,

00:04:50.334 --> 00:04:53.434
I forgot they were stop-motion puppets at one point.

00:04:53.434 --> 00:04:55.200
-Yes. That's the idea.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.901
The idea is for them to become actors.

00:04:58.901 --> 00:05:03.167
Each of these is more expensive than a Hyundai.

00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:04.801
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.801 --> 00:05:06.634
-So they are really mechanically fine.

00:05:06.634 --> 00:05:10.133
-I'm not touching them. -No, you should grab Pinocchio.

00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:14.334
And this guy -- basically what we decided to do

00:05:14.334 --> 00:05:17.267
is act in a naturalistic way.

00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:21.501
Not like a cartoon. Not hyper.

00:05:21.501 --> 00:05:24.334
So you can make them make mistakes.

00:05:24.334 --> 00:05:25.868
When you work with live actors,

00:05:25.868 --> 00:05:28.400
you expect to create something that feels alive.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:31.667
But with a stop-motion puppet, you have to animate mistakes.

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:34.334
So when we see a little footage that I want,

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:36.000
you will see them do "failed acts."

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.400
Stumbling, kicking a bottle that is laying around,

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:41.968
sort of stumbling around.

00:05:41.968 --> 00:05:44.734
And all of that is pre-planned to be animated like that.

00:05:44.734 --> 00:05:46.801
-That's, like, next-level stuff.

00:05:46.801 --> 00:05:49.367
You're like -- Wow. You're making the puppets

00:05:49.367 --> 00:05:51.767
make mistakes to be more human.

00:05:51.767 --> 00:05:53.300
-Yes. Yeah. There is a thing --

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.934
-Hayao Miyazaki, the animator, in Japan said,

00:05:55.934 --> 00:05:59.701
"If you animate the ordinary, it will be extraordinary."

00:05:59.701 --> 00:06:02.567
And the whole movie does that.

00:06:02.567 --> 00:06:03.968
We --

00:06:03.968 --> 00:06:05.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.300
We believe -- Everybody that made this movie

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.667
believes that animation is not a genre just for kids.

00:06:11.667 --> 00:06:14.334
Animation is art. Animation is film.

00:06:14.334 --> 00:06:16.734
And we're trying to make you see these guys

00:06:16.734 --> 00:06:18.701
and say, "They're actors."

00:06:18.701 --> 00:06:20.501
Forget that they're puppets.

00:06:20.501 --> 00:06:23.067
-Yeah, you know, I read one write-up of this

00:06:23.067 --> 00:06:25.067
because I was so excited to have you on the show,

00:06:25.067 --> 00:06:26.734
even before, just looking at a trailer,

00:06:26.734 --> 00:06:30.300
and they said it's your "dark take" on "Pinocchio."

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:31.901
Do you believe that it's a dark take?

00:06:31.901 --> 00:06:33.367
-I think the opposite.

00:06:33.367 --> 00:06:36.434
I find it incredibly beautiful and moving.

00:06:36.434 --> 00:06:39.968
When you see it, you will call your kids or your parents.

00:06:39.968 --> 00:06:43.567
It's incredibly emotional, full of light.

00:06:43.567 --> 00:06:45.834
But it does have the beauty.

00:06:45.834 --> 00:06:48.367
When I saw the first "Pinocchio" with Disney,

00:06:48.367 --> 00:06:50.267
Disney understood one thing --

00:06:50.267 --> 00:06:53.968
that in order to appreciate the light, you need a darkness.

00:06:53.968 --> 00:06:56.634
And so there is a contrasting thing in this movie

00:06:56.634 --> 00:07:00.200
where you will be moved.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.701
And I find myself after all these months of work

00:07:02.701 --> 00:07:07.467
still tearing up watching the movie over and over.

00:07:07.467 --> 00:07:09.434
-It made me cry, as well, but in cheer.

00:07:09.434 --> 00:07:10.901
You just fall in love with it.

00:07:10.901 --> 00:07:15.300
The whole idea of being a boy and living your life.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:16.667
-Yes.

00:07:16.667 --> 00:07:18.767
-There's a poem that you got quoted --

00:07:18.767 --> 00:07:21.934
-Yeah, there is a poem. It's very Mexican.

00:07:21.934 --> 00:07:24.601
But it's about the relationship of life and death.

00:07:24.601 --> 00:07:26.267
Jaime Sabines said,

00:07:26.267 --> 00:07:29.200
"All of my life a voice has whispered softly in my ear,

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:30.834
'Live, live, live.

00:07:30.834 --> 00:07:32.200
It was death."

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:36.567
And the idea of "Pinocchio" is, can we love each other

00:07:36.567 --> 00:07:38.634
while we're briefly in this world?

00:07:38.634 --> 00:07:41.000
Can we belong to each other? Can we support each other?

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:44.267
No matter what you are or what you look like. And that's it.

00:07:44.267 --> 00:07:47.434
The one that learns the lesson in this "Pinocchio" is this guy.

00:07:47.434 --> 00:07:49.767
Geppetto learns to be a real father,

00:07:49.767 --> 00:07:52.534
as opposed to Pinocchio learning to be a real boy.

00:07:52.534 --> 00:07:54.834
[ Cheers and applause ] -I want to show everyone a clip.

00:07:54.834 --> 00:07:56.634
Get ready for this.

00:07:56.634 --> 00:07:58.801
"Guillermo del Toro's Pinocchio."

00:07:58.801 --> 00:08:00.968
Take a look.

00:08:00.968 --> 00:08:04.501
[ Birds chirping ]

00:08:04.501 --> 00:08:06.234
-[ Groans ]

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:09.567
[ Bottle toots ]

00:08:10.968 --> 00:08:12.501
[ Glass shatters ]

00:08:14.734 --> 00:08:16.400
[ Groans ]

00:08:19.567 --> 00:08:21.601
[ Birds chirping ]

00:08:26.701 --> 00:08:28.267
[ Grunts ]

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:52.868
[ Groans ]

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:56.534
-[ Scraping ] -[ Gasps ]

00:08:56.534 --> 00:08:57.701
[ Thud ]

00:08:57.701 --> 00:08:59.367
-[ Laughs ]

00:08:59.367 --> 00:09:01.934
-I mean, wow.

00:09:01.934 --> 00:09:03.968
You just see that. That was a puppet.

00:09:03.968 --> 00:09:07.701
That's amazing. Good job. Good job, Geppetto.

00:09:07.701 --> 00:09:10.100
Guillermo del Toro, everybody.

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:13.300
"Guillermo del Toro's Pinocchio" is in select theaters now

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:15.434
and begins streaming on Netflix this Friday.

00:09:15.434 --> 00:09:17.300
We'll be right back with a performance

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:20.133
from Ashley McBryde and John Osborne. Stick around.

